<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#agora">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Agora</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">An agora was the central public space of an ancient Greek city, used for markets, assemblies and civic life. Greek-style cities with agoras, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatres</a> and gymnasia were founded across Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> after the conquests of Alexander. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>, such cities spread Hellenistic urban culture across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/agora.jpg" alt="Agora" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George E. Koronaios · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Ancient_Agora_from_the_Areopagus_on_May_12,_2020.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Greek word agorá literally means gathering place or assembly. Early in Greek history, from the 10th to the 4th century BCE, free-born citizens gathered in the agora for military duty or to hear statements of the ruling king or council. Later the agora also became a marketplace, where merchants kept stalls or shops amid arcades, and this drew artisans who built workshops nearby. Two Greek verbs came from these twin functions, one political and one commercial: agoreúō, meaning I speak in public, and agorázō, meaning I shop.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The agora was usually placed in the middle of a city or near its harbour, and it was the centre of athletic, artistic, business, social, spiritual and political life. Agoras were built of colonnades, rows of long columns, and contained stoae, long open walkways beneath the colonnades. They were decorated with fountains, trees and statues. When the Athenian agora was rebuilt after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-persian-wars">Greco-Persian Wars</a>, however, colonnades and stoae were not incorporated.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The Ancient Agora of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, below the northern slope of the Acropolis, is the best example. It was the primary meeting ground of the Athenians, where citizens in a democracy gathered to deal with the affairs of the state, where business was conducted, and where people watched performers and listened to philosophers. Religion was central to it. The layout centred on the Panathenaic Way, a road running through the middle of Athens to the city's main gate, the Dipylon, and serving as the route of the Panathenaic festival, held every four years in honour of the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a>.</p><p>The agora housed the Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hephaestus">Hephaestus</a>, the god of metalworking and craftsmen, which survives in good condition. Other temples once stood there to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ares">Ares</a>. The modern term agoraphobia, for anxiety in unfamiliar or open places where the sufferer feels little control, takes its name from the agora as a large, open gathering place.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Agora” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The cities founded by Seleucus I and his successors across Syria, Mesopotamia, Iran and Central Asia introduced Greek civic forms, the <b>agora</b>, the gymnasium and the theatre, into regions with no previous tradition of this kind of civic organisation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>It honoured Harmodius and Aristogeiton with statues in the <b>Agora</b> as the city’s liberators, introduced ostracism, first used in 487 BCE, which could send a citizen who seemed too powerful into exile for ten years, and in 336 BCE passed a law, inscribed on a stone that survives,…</blockquote><a href="/government/tyranny">Tyranny · Impact &amp; Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>According to the informer who betrayed him, Cinadon had pointed out that only about forty of some 4,000 people in the <b>agora</b> were Spartiates, and counted on the hatred of helots, freed helots, inferiors and perioikoi.</blockquote><a href="/societies/sparta">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Sparta</a></li><li><blockquote>Lato's remains include an <b>agora</b>, a prytaneion, a temple and stepped theatral area, showing the civic life of a Cretan polis with kosmoi magistrates.</blockquote><a href="/state/lato">Lato</a></li><li><blockquote>An <b>agora</b> with temples and stoas developed in the 7th century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/megara-hyblaea">Megara Hyblaea</a></li><li><blockquote>Excavations have uncovered its theatre, stadium and <b>agora</b>, among the best-preserved in Greece.</blockquote><a href="/state/messene">Messene</a></li></ul></section>
